tree-sitter-perl: serialize only the quotes that fit

serialize caps the open quotes it writes at 255, the most its one-byte
count can name, but each takes twelve bytes, and 40 bytes of count and
heredoc state go with them. 83 strings nested through interpolation, as
in "@{[ "@{[ 1 ]}" ]}", take 1036 of the 1024 bytes tree-sitter gives a
scanner's state. With its assertions on, tree-sitter's parser aborts the
process on the length the scanner returns; without them, the bytes past
the buffer are overwritten.

The cap counts bytes: as many quotes as fit beside the count and the
heredoc state, 82, are written, and the ones nested deeper are left out,
so their parse degrades to an error.

Applies to src/scanner.c of tree-sitter-perl at commit
0c24d001dd1921e418fb933d208a7bd7dd3f923a, on its release branch.

diff --git a/src/scanner.c b/src/scanner.c
index 7020950d42bf99126f2068947cad3285b7a01013..f3ff91221d303da4691b8139199eec793cc22da9 100644
--- a/src/scanner.c
+++ b/src/scanner.c
@@ -310,10 +310,16 @@ unsigned int tree_sitter_perl_external_scanner_serialize(void *payload, char *bu
   LexerState *state = payload;
   size_t size = 0;
 
-  // Serialize the quotes array
+  // Serialize the quotes array: as many as fit in the buffer beside their
+  // count and the heredoc state after them, and no more than the one byte
+  // holding their count can name
   size_t quote_count = state->quotes.size;
-  if (quote_count > UINT8_MAX) {
-    quote_count = UINT8_MAX;
+  size_t max_quotes = (TREE_SITTER_SERIALIZATION_BUFFER_SIZE - 1 - 3 - sizeof(TSPString)) / sizeof(TSPQuote);
+  if (max_quotes > UINT8_MAX) {
+    max_quotes = UINT8_MAX;
+  }
+  if (quote_count > max_quotes) {
+    quote_count = max_quotes;
   }
   buffer[size++] = (char)quote_count;
 
